WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:04.016
Elena’s music group needs members to prepare before rehearsal.

00:04.413 --> 00:08.112
She proposes a public ranking of reported practice hours.

00:08.520 --> 00:10.229
One member likes the idea.

00:10.612 --> 00:13.724
Another has little time but practises efficiently.

00:14.119 --> 00:18.520
Someone else worries that fewer reported hours will make them look unserious.

00:18.902 --> 00:21.032
Elena wants better playing together.

00:21.415 --> 00:24.872
Hours alone do not establish how well anyone prepared.

00:25.913 --> 00:31.487
Before adding a reward, reminder or pressure, ask what improvement you actually want.

00:31.872 --> 00:36.578
Elena can specify the passage and what ready means, then ask what would help.

00:36.987 --> 00:41.578
If performing requires a minimum preparation level, explain that honestly.

00:41.987 --> 00:46.629
In families, schools and workplaces, some requirements are fixed.

00:46.999 --> 00:52.458
Be clear about what can still be negotiated, such as timing, method or support.

00:53.497 --> 00:56.469
One musician does not know which bars need work.

00:56.878 --> 00:59.110
Another cannot play loudly at home.

00:59.493 --> 01:01.776
Another has the wrong version of the score.

01:02.184 --> 01:04.760
Those problems need different responses.

01:05.156 --> 01:10.028
A reward cannot correct the sheet music, and a detailed plan cannot create time

01:10.041 --> 01:12.107
already needed for paid work or care.

01:12.490 --> 01:14.837
More than one obstacle may be present.

01:15.872 --> 01:19.583
Could reported hours rise while preparation stays the same?

01:19.954 --> 01:24.686
Could public comparison make members reluctant to describe their difficulties?

01:25.056 --> 01:28.385
Discuss the measure with the people who will be judged by it.

01:28.794 --> 01:31.626
The question is not just whether a number changes.

01:32.009 --> 01:35.631
Ask whether it represents the improvement you wanted and what costs

01:35.644 --> 01:37.021
the arrangement creates.

01:38.038 --> 01:43.306
Cerasoli and colleagues combined studies of school, work and physical activities.

01:43.690 --> 01:48.180
Enjoyment was especially linked with performance quality, while incentives were

01:48.192 --> 01:51.088
stronger predictors of quantity in their analysis.

01:51.483 --> 01:54.149
Results varied with the reward arrangement.

01:54.557 --> 01:58.014
This does not mean rewards always help or always harm.

01:58.397 --> 02:02.070
Enjoyment also does not replace fair payment when professional

02:02.083 --> 02:03.537
work is being requested.

02:04.580 --> 02:08.968
Sam says that hours of studying have not stopped them forgetting the material.

02:09.351 --> 02:13.369
Before adding rewards or restrictions, ask how Sam studied.

02:13.752 --> 02:18.650
Dunlosky and colleagues reviewed ten study methods and found broad support for testing

02:18.663 --> 02:21.137
yourself and spreading study across sessions.

02:21.545 --> 02:26.392
One practical option is to close the notes, try questions, check mistakes

02:26.507 --> 02:27.744
and return another day.

02:28.127 --> 02:30.806
It is not a guarantee of particular grades.

02:31.830 --> 02:33.565
Return to the music group.

02:33.974 --> 02:38.897
Study research does not directly establish the best rehearsal method, but Elena can

02:38.910 --> 02:41.576
ask whether members know how to prepare the passage.

02:41.984 --> 02:46.372
One can join a short shared session; another can prepare independently.

02:46.780 --> 02:49.063
Different routes can serve a shared aim.

02:49.459 --> 02:53.311
Make the expectations explicit and agree what happens if someone

02:53.323 --> 02:54.701
cannot prepare in time.

02:55.747 --> 02:58.859
The same care matters in a separate sales example.

02:59.268 --> 03:03.962
A client delaying a software purchase may need budget approval, information about

03:03.975 --> 03:06.819
moving records, or a product that fits better.

03:07.202 --> 03:10.952
Explain costs, limitations and options honestly.

03:11.360 --> 03:15.875
A trial can answer a particular question without proving long-term savings.

03:16.258 --> 03:19.766
Understanding the client may properly lead to no purchase.

03:20.788 --> 03:25.954
Before trying to influence behaviour, ask: What improvement do we actually want?

03:26.325 --> 03:30.483
Check the obstacle, agree support and assess the intended result.

03:30.892 --> 03:32.218
Did the work improve?

03:32.588 --> 03:34.373
Can the commitment continue?

03:34.744 --> 03:36.173
Who did extra work?

03:36.542 --> 03:41.223
Check your part too: whether you provided the instructions and help you promised.

03:41.593 --> 03:46.044
Cooperation needs a workable arrangement, not just compliance once.
